            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Weekly Market Update</h2>
<p>Multiple overseas teams reported declining ROI in Southeast Asia and Latin America ad delivery this week. After diagnosing nearly twenty accounts, problems concentrated on localization execution. This week we focus on this high-frequency pain point, outlining five typical mistakes.</p>
<h2>Mistake 1: Translation Instead of Localization</h2>
<p>This is the easiest mistake to make. A utility App used machine-translated copy directly in the Vietnamese market, with "Download now to solve your problems" sounding too direct and lacking warmth in Vietnamese context. The correct approach is having native market creators polish the copy, not simple translation.</p>
<p><strong>Suggestion</strong>: Establish a localization review process; ad copy must be tested by target market users before official launch.</p>
<h2>Mistake 2: Payment Methods Misaligned</h2>
<p>Southeast Asia still has many users relying on convenience store cash payments and local e-wallets. Direct credit card page redirects cause high cart abandonment. One cross-border e-commerce team lost nearly 30% of potential orders in a single week due to broken payment chains. Latin America is similar, with Brazil's Boleto and Mexico's OXXO coverage far exceeding international payment tools.</p>
<p><strong>Suggestion</strong>: Adapt to local mainstream payment methods on both account recharge and user payment ends; test the complete chain before billing.</p>
<h2>Mistake 3: Copying Account Structures from Mature Markets</h2>
<p>Some teams directly apply European and American advertising account structures to Southeast Asia and Latin America without distinguishing budget allocation and bidding strategies. In reality, emerging markets need longer learning periods and lower single-account budgets to avoid single-account volatility affecting overall delivery rhythm.</p>
<p><strong>Suggestion</strong>: Set up independent account groups for different markets, with budgets controlled at around 60% of mature markets; observe for two weeks before adjustments.</p>
<h2>Mistake 4: Not Differentiating Materials by Market</h2>
<p>Using one version of video material simultaneously across multiple countries seems efficient but is actually ineffective. A casual game team discovered the same ad creative had click rates in Indonesia only half that in the Philippines. The issue lies in background music rhythm, skin tone of characters, scene settings, and other details.</p>
<p><strong>Suggestion</strong>: Prepare at least two sets of localized materials for each core market, including local language voiceovers and native scene elements.</p>
<h2>Mistake 5: Ignoring Cultural Taboos and Regulatory Differences</h2>
<p>Two cases this week deserve vigilance. One team delivered creatives containing pork elements in a predominantly Muslim market; another team launched at scale in a Latin American country without completing ad content pre-review. These operations range from platform rejection to triggering account risk controls or even legal risks.</p>
<p><strong>Suggestion</strong>: Establish taboo word lists and content compliance checklists for target markets; have colleagues familiar with local markets review before launch.</p>
<h2>This Week's Action Items</h2>
<ol>
<li>Check existing ad accounts for the above five issues</li>
<li>List high-priority materials requiring localization improvements</li>
<li>Communicate with recharge suppliers to confirm target market mainstream payment method support</li>
<li>Develop this week's localization improvement plan</li>
</ol>
<p>Localization is not a one-time task but a systematic project requiring continuous optimization.</p>
